<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Este, Veneto</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ateste</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Este is a town in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>, north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, at the foot of the Euganean Hills. As Ateste it was a leading centre of the ancient Veneti, giving its name to the Este culture of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, and its museum holds rich finds of bronze situlae and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetic">Venetic</a> inscriptions. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was the seat of the family that took its name, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-este">House of Este</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/este-veneto.jpg" alt="Este, Veneto" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Inviaggio at Italian Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Este_castello.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
